A||||10||Pike||I'm ready to give up!!!||||1||
Z||000000||Pike||10-04-2001||01:31 AM||||Can ANYBODY help me? 87 GT 2.8 Auto A/C. 126K miles. On the way to work...Engine just Quit, it would crank but no pop....3 months ago. I pulled the Engine and did a rebuild always expecting to find the reason for the engine failure...I never did find any problem in the engine...a ton of time,money and work for noth'in. I put the new engine back in the car 2 weeks ago. And guess what...the stink'in thing has the same problem. It cranks but will not fire. This is what I have done over the last 2 weeks to troubleshoot the problem.<P>new coil<BR>new distributer module<BR>all plugs, plug wires etc. are brand new of course<BR>fuel pump pressure 45 PSI<BR>Timeing set to no.1 TDC with timeing mark on "0" degrees<BR>All plugs spark<BR>all cylinders get fuel<BR>Valve lash adjusted according to manual<BR>Checked computer<BR>Fireing order 123456<P>What can be wrong with this stink'in piece of $%#@. This is my transportation vehicle and I MUST get it running. Can anyone help me?<BR>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-04-2001||01:46 AM||||Welcome to the forum....<P>Edit...just reread your list.<P>You have a lot of new parts...but how about the magnetic pickup in the distributor?  I know you say you have spark, but is it consistant?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-04-2001||02:18 AM||||1 Spark<BR>2 Fuel<BR>3 Compression<BR>4 Air<BR>These are the 4 things an engine needs to run. Its obviously one of these. The fact that you rebuilt it and it does the same thing leads me to believe that it is something other than the actual engine itself. <BR>Fuel pump regulator perhaps?<BR>One of the gillion sensors? (Did you replace ALL the sensors?)<BR>Computer?<BR>Clogged fuel line?<BR>Air restriction?<BR>No CONSTANT spark?<BR>Just a thought...........<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="blue"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||10-04-2001||05:38 AM||||Timing should be set at 11 degrees BTDC not Zero.<BR>If your getting fuel and spark you should get some kind of fire even if it doesn't crank. Nothing at all?<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan87GT||10-04-2001||07:15 AM||||Pike,<BR>If you look at some of my postings over the last week I had the same trouble the last few weeks. The porblem was that I set the valve lash according to the Haynes manual, 1-1/2 turns after you can't turn the push rods between your fingers. I ended up checking the compression and it was zero. Backed the rocker nuts off 1 turn and I had compression...||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-04-2001||09:50 AM||||Are you getting any "error" codes? If so, what are they? If you let it sit for a little while (hour, day, week), does it fire and run again? Have you changed/checked your fuel pump relay? <P>You can pin-point the fuel system with a can of starting fluid. Next time it dies and won't start, remove the intake hose from the throttle body and squirt starting fluid into the throttle body and crank. If the motor fires and dies, the problem is fuel related. If it doesn't fire, its ignition related.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000006||wkayl||10-04-2001||10:35 AM||||Welcome to the world of pulling your hair out.  I went throught the same situation for the last two to three weeks.  Is there anyone in your area that can help you.  Mrfiero came over last Sunday and we spent 2 hours checking (and double checking) everything again.  Even though I replaced my dist and had the module checked by an auto parts store, it ended up still being a bad module.  Check to malke sure you have spark out of the coil (stick a spark plug in coil wire end that normaly goes to dist cap).  have someone crank.  If no spark, check to make sure 12 volts going to coil with key on(grey connector).  I know some of this sounds redundant but check pickup coil with an ohm meeter and all electrical connections in and around dist.  Do a search on my name (wkayl) and you will find the posts that helped me.  Topic was "No Spark".<P>Good luck<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/usa1017[1].gif">   <BR>Dnyee's 88 Formula, Casey's 86GT, Wayne's 86GT.  <BR>We consider my wife as being "Fiero Challenged"||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pike||10-04-2001||10:03 PM||||OK Gang. Now add to the list a new computer. What a waste. Still will not fire. I have now pulled the intake manifold and valve covers and I think I've discovered what may be the problem Haynes Vlave lash adjustment. Compression check showed about 90 lbs. I thought that with unseated rings on a brand new engine this might be OK? I backed off the adjustment nut on the rockers and took another compression reading. I gained 40 lbs! Think this might be the problem? Well If the Haynes manual is wrong and it vcertainly looks that way...what is the right way to set lash?||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||10-04-2001||11:08 PM||||I believe the consensus is to set valve lash 1/2 turn instead of 1 1/2...<P>On the chance that's not the problem. Many years ago I had a Chevy 327 that was almost impossible to start. I had to twist the distributor, pour gas in the carb and still took a while. It finally got where it wouldn't start at all and the starter started dragging after the first few tries. I replaced the starter and it would start first time, every time. Apparently the starter was turning it fast enough to SOUND like it was OK but not quite fast enough to start it. Just a thought...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-04-2001||11:18 PM||||I think people miss understand the valve lash asjustment. I heard it said that you turned the nut 1-1/2 turn past once you could no longer turn the pushrod. You don't want it tight enough to the point you can't rotate the rod, thats way to tight, then plus another 1.5 turns! Zero last is when play is removed from the up and down movement of the pushrod, not really twirling it in your fingers...<P>To do it, I used about 3/4 turn on my new engine. Just twirl the push rod between your fingers while tightening the nut, once you feel a tiny amount of drag that is zero lash, then turn the nut 3/4 of a turn more, the extra turn are just to center the hole in the middle of the rod to the middle of the lifter plunger. You want to do it when the lifter is down, or in the closed position for the valve...<P>90 psi compresion should be good enough to make the engine run fair. Get yourself a fuel pressure guage and check your fuel pressure at the rail..I'm betting its a fuel delievery problem myself...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pike||10-04-2001||11:52 PM||||OK Guys & Gals...Here we go. I found the problem!!! And boy do I feel like a turd! 2 problems found: 1st. I had the timeing 180 degrees out. I don't know how I did it...but I did it. 2nd: My valve adjustment was wayyyy off. You'd have thought I'd been able to do this correctly while the engine was out of the car and on the bench...but I guess I'm an idiot. I've done many other engines before in my life...so, I can't tell you why I screwed it up for sure... but I can tell you this much, the Haynes manual really had me confused on which cylinder was No. 1. The little picture in the book shows the distributer end of the engine as "Front" and I remember cavitating a couple of times on which cyl. was no. 1. I knew the process I read the stink'in Haynes manual (which is now in the trash)So anyway...is this the part where I put it all back together and it still won't run? Man! I sure hope not. Oh, by the way...I checked pressure out of the fuel pump and I get around 45 PSI  and I think this is correct...Thanks to everybody who lent a helping hand...I really appreciate it and I'll keep you all posted on my progress with the car...it's such a purdy little thing! I shure hope I can get this figured out.<BR>Regards,<BR>Pike<BR>PS&gt;found the oil leak! cabin side valve cover gasket. Hooooya!!!||||reg||1
